{
  "id": "2026-10-02-dnd-library-choice",
  "scope": "dnd",
  "kind": "review",
  "date": "2026-10-02",
  "question": "Which native drag owner and UI policies should block dragging and drop indicators reuse?",
  "model": "Codex",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v3",
  "trigger": "The user requested a Best API Review of React DnD versus current libraries and other editors, then asked whether a library supplies full coverage/testing and why DndKit remains after transfer moved into Plite/core. Assessment and research only.",
  "requirements": [
    "Plite remains the canonical owner of complete transfers, exact source/view/root authority, schema admission, lossless move/refusal, copy intent, selection and history.",
    "Preserve native text, block and file/data transfer, same-window foreign formats, mounted iframe editors, and exactly one recipient across nested views.",
    "Keep optional copied handles, menus, preview styling, indicators and accessible alternatives configurable without installing a second document-transfer engine.",
    "Delegate browser mechanics only when the completed integration removes maintenance responsibility and preserves selection/repair/scroll ordering.",
    "Do not treat library tests, source inspection, desktop browser tests or existing development-build benchmarks as full editor, mobile, OS or production proof.",
    "Compare modern dnd-kit separately from legacy core, retain inspected-source revisions and prior decisions, and leave product code unchanged."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Reconciled the October 1 native-transfer and interrogation reviews, design and amended design, and the October 2 execution with partial proof. Inspected current Plite DOM/session and copied registry responsibilities. Reused the earlier native-versus-React-DnD development benchmark only within its recorded limits. Three independent same-family researchers inspected three library and five editor repositories; the lead checked 43 selected files against their commit bytes. Three fresh-context same-family reviewers challenged the direction and added iframe and same-window custom-MIME gates plus the cutBlocks ownership qualification. No runtime suite or benchmark was rerun.",
  "summary": "Defer replacing the adopted native driver with Pragmatic Drag and Drop pending one bounded integration and production comparison. Keep the current native driver now; reject React DnD reintroduction and modern dnd-kit as default replacements for the existing job. PDD is the strongest maintenance candidate because it owns native browser quirks, but source anchors, MIME capture, exact mounted-view routing, transfer admission and scheduler coordination remain editor responsibilities. The copied DndKit is optional product UI and action wiring, unrelated to npm dnd-kit; retain it while recording its shared cutBlocks helper as a clipboard ownership concern. No library supplies full editor coverage. Only the browser-driver question reopens; transfer semantics and ownership stay settled.",
  "verdict": "defer",
  "callSites": {
    "current": "Native DragEvent -> Plite DOM source/landing/session -> Plite transfer or external import. Copied DndKit handles and move actions call dom.drag.start and api.transfer.move. Plite owns document, schema, selection and history.",
    "proposed": "Provisional private PDD native lifecycle -> Plite source view/keys/range plus full MIME, landing and one recipient -> unchanged transfer/import API. Delete generic token/session routing only after replacement proof. Retain optional copied controls; do not add a public backend abstraction."
  },
  "previous": "2026-10-01-dnd-transfer-interrogation",
  "relation": "defers",
