  "alternatives": [
    "Keep native: retain as the measured baseline. It preserves one current native path but leaves browser quirks and lifecycle maintenance with Plite; source inspection does not establish it as the permanent winner.",
    "Replace generic native lifecycle with private Pragmatic Drag and Drop: strongest alternative, deferred. Test source range capture, iframe mounting, same-window foreign custom MIME, one nested-view recipient and cleanup before matched production scale. Adopt only for real responsibility deletion with no material behavior/cost regression; speedup is not required.",
    "Replace the default driver with modern @dnd-kit/dom: reject for the current job. Pointer/touch/keyboard sensors are useful, but the inspected native driver is incomplete/unexported and external text/files still require native transport; feedback, selection and scrolling introduce coordination. Reopen for a demonstrated independent pointer/touch need.",
    "Reintroduce React DnD: reject. HTML5 payload support does not eliminate editor transport/semantics, touch remains a separate backend, and its keyboard option only handles cancellation. An actual application-manager integration could change this question.",
    "Delete DndKit or move copied controls into Plite core: reject. The optional registry preset still installs handles, indicator, menus and shortcuts. It is not a third-party backend. Its shared asynchronous cutBlocks helper is clipboard action orchestration and merits separate ownership scrutiny, not a new drag engine.",
    "Import a library solely to inherit full coverage: reject the premise. Upstream unit/browser tests cover delegated unchanged mechanics; core, mounted editor, native device/application and copied interaction proof remain ours."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "Source comparison only. No product or dependency change, upstream suite, editor test, browser interaction, device test, production build or new benchmark ran. Selected editor clones predate current remote tips; only stated commits were inspected. The Slate checkout is a fork and ProseMirror GitHub differs from its canonical forge. PDD's inspected native text-selection Playwright case skips non-Firefox browsers; peer editor tests also have scoped skips. Existing native benchmark uses development builds and does not compare PDD or modern dnd-kit. A concurrent session is repairing transfer validation; this review does not certify that repair. The initial global ledger check failed on concurrent unmapped scratch probes; the same HEAD check passed in a detached worktree. The remaining browser/zz-word-dnd2.probe was mapped to DnD source membership only, not used as test proof. Refresh/render/check then passed. No production performance, mobile/OS parity or full-coverage claim.",
